<http://data.silknow.org/event/4957e424-d5c9-39ac-809f-c563c2823983>
        a                              ecrm:E8_Acquisition ;
        rdfs:comment                   "This silk playbill was produced to mark the opening of His Majesty's Theatre, Richmond, for the seventh season under the management of Mr. William Sidney, whose first season as lessee began on the 16th August 1858, after the freehold had been purchased by the Queen and it had become Crown property.  The original theatre on the site opened on 15 June 1765 as The New Theatre on Richmond Green, by popular demand since Richmond was a favourite place for visitors, and where many 'gentry and nobility' lived who wanted a theatre.  The old theatre on Richmond Hill was falling down and only gave irregular performances.  The theatre had several lessees between 1765 and 1858, including Edmund Kean, and it was closed from 1845 until 1849.  William Sidney, lessee, actor and manager, ran the theatre for twelve or thirteen years with his wife, and did a lot to improve the reputation of the theatre.  He got star performers from the central London theatres to perform there, including Madame Celeste, Hermann Vezin, and E.H. Sothern.  The principal 'low comedian' of his company was Arthur Williams, who featured in these plays on the bill as Captain Ratts in <i>Octoroon; or Life in Louisiana</i>, and Isaac in <i>The Maid and the Magpie</i>."@en ;
        ecrm:P22_transferred_title_to  <http://data.silknow.org/actor/f0577f91-f887-3019-bf88-f9e5ba019390> ;
        ecrm:P24_transferred_title_of  <http://data.silknow.org/object/7d200ecb-416f-33e5-b1a2-1e502080ebea> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "This silk playbill was produced to mark the opening of His Majesty's Theatre, Richmond, for the seventh season under the management of Mr. William Sidney, whose first season as lessee began on the 16th August 1858, after the freehold had been purchased by the Queen and it had become Crown property.  The original theatre on the site opened on 15 June 1765 as The New Theatre on Richmond Green, by popular demand since Richmond was a favourite place for visitors, and where many 'gentry and nobility' lived who wanted a theatre.  The old theatre on Richmond Hill was falling down and only gave irregular performances.  The theatre had several lessees between 1765 and 1858, including Edmund Kean, and it was closed from 1845 until 1849.  William Sidney, lessee, actor and manager, ran the theatre for twelve or thirteen years with his wife, and did a lot to improve the reputation of the theatre.  He got star performers from the central London theatres to perform there, including Madame Celeste, Hermann Vezin, and E.H. Sothern.  The principal 'low comedian' of his company was Arthur Williams, who featured in these plays on the bill as Captain Ratts in <i>Octoroon; or Life in Louisiana</i>, and Isaac in <i>The Maid and the Magpie</i>."@en .

